sdio: give artosyn_sdio_write() a real (patient) wait instead of a
5*10-jiffy timeout that always falls back to ~100ms polling

Depends on 0010/0011 (numbered after them; touches the same function
0011 documents in its own background section).

Background: bringing up the SDIO daemon (ar8030d -i 1, USING_8030SDIO=ON
-- see 0011's own patch) measured real, sustained throughput far below
even this project's own DRV-mode baseline, let alone the vendor's
~18-25Mbps. The daemon's own debug-pad log (/var/log/ar8030/daemon_log/,
dev_dat_so_write_proc's "send ok"/"send cpl" entries) showed consecutive
writes completing in a clean ~100-110ms cadence (110/220/330/440ms
gaps) regardless of chunk size -- the signature of a fixed per-write
round-trip cost, not a genuine bandwidth limit.

Root cause: artosyn_sdio_write()'s wait for write_condition (write_offset
< write_valid_size, set by the real TX-ready mailbox interrupt) only
ever waited 5 * 10 jiffies (well under a second total at typical HZ)
before giving up and falling back to WORKAROUND_FOR_INTERRUPT_LOST_ISSUE's
own polling path (oal_queue_work() -> ar_sdio_reg_check(), on its own
workqueue thread). Decompiling the vendor's own artosyn_sdio.ko (Ghidra,
via ghidra-mcp) for comparison -- its artosyn_write() ioctl path calls an
equivalent write function that waits via a plain prepare_to_wait_event()/
schedule()/finish_wait() loop with no per-iteration timeout at all: it
just blocks, patiently, until the real interrupt wakes it (confirmed:
same write_offset/write_valid_size state, same condition check, only the
wait strategy differs). The real TX-ready interrupt does fire in this
driver -- artosyn_sdio_irqhandler()'s tx-ready branch already calls
wake_up_interruptible_all(&dev->tx_q) correctly -- our own wait just
never gave it enough time to arrive before bailing out to the much
slower polling fallback, on nearly every single write.

Fix: replace the 5-iteration short-timeout loop with one
wait_event_interruptible_timeout() call using a much longer timeout
(SDIO_WRITE_WAIT_MS, 2000ms) -- patient like the vendor's design, but
still bounded (unlike upstream's literal forever) as a safety net
against a genuinely wedged link. Keeps a single courtesy
oal_queue_work(reg_workqueue) kick before the wait, in case the
interrupt that would satisfy the condition was already missed before
this call started (same reasoning as the old retry loop's repeated
kicks, just no longer needed every ~10ms since the wait itself now
relies on the real wakeup).

artosyn_read() was compared the same way (the vendor's own read is the
identical prepare_to_wait_event()/schedule() pattern) but deliberately
left alone here: unlike write, it already checks read_valid_size/
read_offset directly against a return of -EAGAIN before ever entering
its wait loop, and by the time this driver's own chardev read() is
called at all, the daemon's own sdio_ev_loop() has already gated it
behind a successful poll() -- the debug-pad log showed no equivalent
~100ms-per-read stall on this path, so there was no measured problem to
fix. Not yet re-tested on real hardware as of this patch.

--- a/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
+++ b/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
@@ -830,10 +830,28 @@
     return ret;
 }
 
+/* Ghidra-decompiled comparison against the vendor's own artosyn_sdio.ko
+ * (artosyn_write() -> a FUN_000106de() equivalent to this function) found
+ * that its wait for write_condition is a plain, effectively-unbounded
+ * prepare_to_wait_event()/schedule()/finish_wait() loop -- no short
+ * per-iteration timeout at all, just a patient block until the real
+ * mailbox interrupt (artosyn_sdio_irqhandler()'s tx-ready branch) wakes
+ * it. This function's own wait used to bail out after a mere 5 * 10
+ * jiffies (well under a second total at typical HZ) and fall back to
+ * polling ar_sdio_reg_check() via the workqueue -- confirmed live via the
+ * daemon's own debug-pad log (dev_dat_so_write_proc's "send ok"/"send
+ * cpl" timestamps clustering in ~100ms multiples) that this fallback
+ * path, not the real interrupt, was satisfying nearly every single
+ * write, capping achievable throughput at roughly (chunk size / 100ms)
+ * regardless of payload size or link quality. SDIO_WRITE_WAIT_MS below
+ * is a single, much longer wait matching the vendor's patience (still
+ * bounded, unlike upstream's literal forever, as a safety net against a
+ * genuinely wedged link). */
+#define SDIO_WRITE_WAIT_MS 2000
+
 static ssize_t artosyn_sdio_write(struct artosyn_sdio_device *dev, uint8_t* buf, size_t count, bool non_block)
 {
     int err = -1;
-    int retry_count = 5;
     size_t count_bak = 0;
     unsigned int valid_size_bak = 0;
     ssize_t ret = 0;
@@ -869,22 +887,22 @@
     if (!dev->rom_mode) {
         AR_DBG(DM_SDIO, DL_INF, "%s: write wait\n", __func__);
 
-        do {
-            err = wait_event_interruptible_timeout(dev->tx_q, artosyn_sdio_write_condition(dev), 10);
-            if (err > 0) {
-                break;
-            }
-
 #ifdef WORKAROUND_FOR_INTERRUPT_LOST_ISSUE
-            // check reg
-            if (dev->handle) {
-                oal_queue_work(dev->handle, dev->reg_workqueue);
-            }
+        /* One courtesy poll before the real wait, in case the mailbox
+         * interrupt that would satisfy write_condition() below already
+         * came and went (missed) before we got here -- same reasoning
+         * as upstream's own retry loop, just no longer repeated every
+         * ~10ms now that the wait itself is patient enough to rely on
+         * the real wakeup instead. */
+        if (dev->handle) {
+            oal_queue_work(dev->handle, dev->reg_workqueue);
+        }
 #endif
-            retry_count--;
-        } while((err <= 0) && (retry_count > 0));
 
-        if (retry_count == 0)
+        err = wait_event_interruptible_timeout(dev->tx_q, artosyn_sdio_write_condition(dev),
+                                                msecs_to_jiffies(SDIO_WRITE_WAIT_MS));
+
+        if (err <= 0)
         {
             AR_DBG(DM_SDIO, DL_ERR, "%s: write wait timeout\n", __func__);
             sdio_stats_print();//zy test
